}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
The link with matrix powers is the weighted-walk identity. For a directed \
walk w = (i\:2080, \[Ellipsis], i\:2096), let wt(w) be the product of the \
matrix entries corresponding to its successive edges. Then\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"df8b2acc-cb56-6140-aec1-93fd62ce6a03"],

Cell[BoxData[
 FormBox[
  RowBox[{
   SubscriptBox[
    RowBox[{"(", 
     SuperscriptBox["B", "k"], ")"}], "ij"], "=", 
   UnderscriptBox["\[Sum]", 
    RowBox[{"w", ":", "i", "\[Rule]", "j", ",", 
     RowBox[{"|", "w", "|"}], "=", "k"}]], 
   RowBox[{"wt", "(", "w", ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormula",Expres\
sionUUID->"8a02cf35-6377-474f-b213-63c73c85c545"],

Cell["\<\
Here k \[GreaterEqual] 1 and |w| denotes the number of edges, with \
repetitions permitted. This is the usual matrix-product expansion, organised \
by walks in G(B).\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"10b6966d-51de-6947-be2d-c0e6856915de"],

Cell["\<\
In M, the length-three closed walks based at vertex 1 are 1 \[RightArrow] 2 \
\[RightArrow] 3 \[RightArrow] 1 and 1 \[RightArrow] 3 \[RightArrow] 2 \
\[RightArrow] 1, with weights -4 and +4. Thus the (1,1) entry of M\.b3 is \
zero. The full matrix computation above verifies cancellation in every entry \
of the cube.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"15a622a3-f0bc-2543-8ee5-df9bfb5fde7d"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Consequences for prescribed patterns", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"98f606f8-34cb-a048-bf11-61fa221d0634"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Acyclic pattern. ",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "If G(B) is acyclic on n vertices, it has no walk of length n. The \
weighted-walk identity therefore gives B\:207f = 0, independently of the \
signs of the nonzero entries. In particular, G(U) is the chain 1 \
\[RightArrow] 2 \[RightArrow] 3."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"4bed3ae7-a117-034e-9826-5e53165ee324"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Nonnegative case. ",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "If B is entrywise nonnegative and G(B) contains a directed cycle of length \
l and weight c > 0 through i, repeating that cycle gives the bound below for \
every positive integer r."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"ae865fc3-1971-f845-a08d-a35e2f9d4d58"],

Cell[BoxData[
 FormBox[
  RowBox[{
   SubscriptBox[
    RowBox[{"(", 
     SuperscriptBox["B", "rl"], ")"}], "ii"], "\[GreaterEqual]", 
   SuperscriptBox["c", "r"], ">", "0"}], TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormula",E\
xpressionUUID->"49fe914f-d78a-ea49-a868-4aa117e6e7b9"],

Cell["\<\
Hence B has nonzero powers of arbitrarily large order and cannot be \
nilpotent. Together with the acyclic case, this proves that an entrywise \
nonnegative matrix is nilpotent if and only if its directed nonzero pattern \
is acyclic.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"1bae00bb-490a-eb40-b0f2-21396743778d"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Signed case. ",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "Negative walk weights invalidate the positivity argument. The matrix M \
exhibits this obstruction: its graph identifies the available cycle products, \
while their values determine cancellation. Thus the graph supplies structural \
restrictions on a prescribed pattern, rather than a nilpotency test for \
arbitrary signed matrices."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"5425b9db-de98-8c42-82b8-4cd70678ed0b"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["6. Nonzero diagonal and a smaller nilpotency index", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"09f5bff1-3f01-f24d-acbd-4a03c75b309a"],

Cell["\<\
The next example N has a = 1 and e = -2. It demonstrates that a signed \
persymmetric nilpotent matrix may have nonzero diagonal entries and \
nilpotency index 2.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"27487217-07e5-0a4e-b313-b6bb78f91e79"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"rankOneExample", " ", "=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"1", ",", " ", "1", ",", " ", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"-", "1"}], "/", "2"}]}], "}"}], ",", " ", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"-", "2"}], ",", " ", 
       RowBox[{"-", "2"}], ",", " ", "1"}], "}"}], ",", " ", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"-", "2"}], ",", " ", 
       RowBox[{"-", "2"}], ",", " ", "1"}], "}"}]}], "}"}]}], ";", " ", 
  RowBox[{"{", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"MatrixForm", "[", "rankOneExample", "]"}], ",", " ", 
    RowBox[{"Simplify", "[", 
     RowBox[{"rankOneExample", " ", "==", " ", 
      RowBox[{"j3", ".", 
       RowBox[{"Transpose", "[", "rankOneExample", "]"}], ".", "j3"}]}], 
     "]"}], ",", " ", 
    RowBox[{"Expand", "[", 
     RowBox[{"Det", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"x", " ", 
        RowBox[{"IdentityMatrix", "[", "3", "]"}]}], " ", "-", " ", 
       "rankOneExample"}], "]"}], "]"}], ",", " ", 
    RowBox[{"MatrixRank", "[", "rankOneExample", "]"}], ",", " ", 
    RowBox[{"MatrixForm", "[", 
     RowBox[{"MatrixPower", "[", 
      RowBox[{"rankOneExample", ",", " ", "2"}], "]"}], "]"}]}], 
   "}"}]}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[72]:=",ExpressionUUID->"5cb63d83-1d21-7c4e-8757-768dd7dc1cd5"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   TagBox[
    RowBox[{"(", "\[NoBreak]", GridBox[{
       {"1", "1", 
        RowBox[{"-", 
         FractionBox["1", "2"]}]},
       {
        RowBox[{"-", "2"}], 
        RowBox[{"-", "2"}], "1"},
       {
        RowBox[{"-", "2"}], 
        RowBox[{"-", "2"}], "1"}
      },
      GridBoxAlignment->{"Columns" -> {{Center}}, "Rows" -> {{Baseline}}},
      GridBoxSpacings->{"Columns" -> {
          Offset[0.27999999999999997`], {
           Offset[0.7]}, 
          Offset[0.27999999999999997`]}, "Rows" -> {
          Offset[0.2], {
           Offset[0.4]}, 
          Offset[0.2]}}], "\[NoBreak]", ")"}],
    Function[BoxForm`e$, 
     MatrixForm[BoxForm`e$]]], ",", "True", ",", 
   SuperscriptBox["x", "3"], ",", "1", ",", 
   TagBox[
    RowBox[{"(", "\[NoBreak]", GridBox[{
       {"0", "0", "0"},
       {"0", "0", "0"},
       {"0", "0", "0"}
      },
      GridBoxAlignment->{"Columns" -> {{Center}}, "Rows" -> {{Baseline}}},
      GridBoxSpacings->{"Columns" -> {
          Offset[0.27999999999999997`], {
           Offset[0.7]}, 
          Offset[0.27999999999999997`]}, "Rows" -> {
          Offset[0.2], {
           Offset[0.4]}, 
          Offset[0.2]}}], "\[NoBreak]", ")"}],
    Function[BoxForm`e$, 
     MatrixForm[BoxForm`e$]]]}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.9994122651792088`*^9, 3.999433780244669*^9},
 CellLabel->"Out[72]=",ExpressionUUID->"9a2733d5-0018-5f44-a609-5280ffe6630d"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
The rank-one structure explains the vanishing square: N = uv\:1d40 with u = \
(1, -2, -2)\:1d40 and v = (1, 1, -1/2)\:1d40. Since v\:1d40u = 0, we have N\
\.b2 = u(v\:1d40u)v\:1d40 = 0. As N \[NotEqual] 0, its minimal polynomial is \
x\.b2, although its characteristic polynomial remains x\.b3.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"57ac08ba-7bc0-8c48-9669-c5cc8f2bd35b"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["7. Reality of eigenvalues is not automatic", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"38879280-2b72-eb4a-a89e-734374c64e6a"],

Cell["\<\
The following matrix is both persymmetric and real skew-symmetric. Its \
characteristic polynomial x(x\.b2 + 2) gives the spectrum {0, i\[Sqrt]2, -i\
\[Sqrt]2}, demonstrating that real persymmetry alone does not imply a real \
spectrum.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"4e27494f-ea34-9a4f-9125-f941e01443f2"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"complexExample", " ", "=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"0", ",", " ", "1", ",", " ", "0"}], "}"}], ",", " ", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"-", "1"}], ",", " ", "0", ",", " ", "1"}], "}"}], ",", " ", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"0", ",", " ", 
       RowBox[{"-", "1"}], ",", " ", "0"}], "}"}]}], "}"}]}], ";", " ", 
  RowBox[{"{", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"Simplify", "[", 
     RowBox[{"complexExample", " ", "==", " ", 
      RowBox[{"j3", ".", 
       RowBox[{"Transpose", "[", "complexExample", "]"}], ".", "j3"}]}], 
     "]"}], ",", " ", 
    RowBox[{"Factor", "[", 
     RowBox[{"Det", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"x", " ", 
        RowBox[{"IdentityMatrix", "[", "3", "]"}]}], " ", "-", " ", 
       "complexExample"}], "]"}], "]"}], ",", " ", 
    RowBox[{"Eigenvalues", "[", "complexExample", "]"}]}], "}"}]}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[73]:=",ExpressionUUID->"9f995ad1-d0d0-f54d-bdf0-f10476400b62"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"True", ",", 
   RowBox[{"x", " ", 
    RowBox[{"(", 
     RowBox[{"2", "+", 
      SuperscriptBox["x", "2"]}], ")"}]}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"\[ImaginaryI]", " ", 
      SqrtBox["2"]}], ",", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"-", "\[ImaginaryI]"}], " ", 
      SqrtBox["2"]}], ",", "0"}], "}"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.9994122651832104`*^9, 3.999433780249668*^9},
 CellLabel->"Out[73]=",ExpressionUUID->"f50a0b80-2059-9847-b14d-173060026cb5"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["8. Nonnegative specialization", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.999434981342079*^9, 
  3.999434981513092*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"dd8c56d4-15cc-064e-9626-\
e73e5b9eb3a8"],

Cell["\<\
Suppose A is entrywise nonnegative and \[Chi](x) = x\.b3. The trace condition \
gives a = e = 0. The remaining equations are 2bc + df = 0 and b\.b2f + c\.b2d \
= 0; nonnegativity therefore yields\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"075a7e9a-0413-7843-a51d-010fb735c1a4"],

Cell["a = e = 0,     bc = df = 0,     b\.b2f = c\.b2d = 0.", "DisplayFormula",ExpressionUUID->"523d14e3-bc76-d440-a5cd-75de5c91e5b9"],

Cell["\<\
Conversely, these equalities force \[Chi](x) = x\.b3. They eliminate loops, \
directed 2-cycles and directed 3-cycles, respectively, giving the entrywise \
version of the acyclicity criterion in this dimension. For signed entries, \
the coefficient equations instead permit cancellation between the \
corresponding products.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"0effdb26-3eba-bb4d-9701-cb86324200b1"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]]
},
